service.py,baymax-backend/server/service.py,f634db601964809d0fc963aa2e43f91161a7ec9c,joohyeon/baymax_team_collab,https://api.github.com/repositories/961077303/contents/baymax-backend/server/service.py?ref=f92a5e91b09e4b4800bc57c1b012f281202f91d3,https://api.github.com/repositories/961077303/git/blobs/f634db601964809d0fc963aa2e43f91161a7ec9c,https://github.com/joohyeon/baymax_team_collab/blob/f92a5e91b09e4b4800bc57c1b012f281202f91d3/baymax-backend/server/service.py,agentx hackathon team collaboration ,,0,0,6,111,2025-05-23T14:46:39Z,2,0,"# Conversational AI Agent for Symptom Documentation 

This project implements a conversational AI agent that conducts interactive symptom documentation using structured memory. The agent collects and iteratively updates symptom details based on a predefined schema, guiding the user through a dynamic dialogue to fill in missing information.

## Project Structure

This project is organized into two main components:

- **baymax-backend/**: Backend Python application with the conversational AI agent
- **baymax-frontend/**: Expo frontend application

## Participation in AgentX Challenge and Berkeley MOOC

This project is part of our submission to the [AgentX Challenge](https://rdi.berkeley.edu/agentx/), hosted by the [Berkeley Advanced Agentic AI MOOC](https://llmagents-learning.org/sp25). 

## Architecture

The system is built using Python with LangChain and LangGraph frameworks, and consists of the following components:

### Core Components

1. **SymptomState** (`baymax-backend/symptom_state.py`):
   - Manages the state of the conversation including primary symptoms, symptom details, and follow-up questions
   - Provides methods for adding symptoms, updating details, and identifying missing fields
   - Handles multi-symptom management and session summaries

2. **Conversation Agent** (`baymax-backend/conversation_agent.py`):
   - Implements the main conversation flow as a LangGraph
   - Handles user input processing and agent response generation
   - Orchestrates the extraction and documentation of symptom details

3. **Thread Management System** (`baymax-backend/conversation_thread.py`):
   - Tracks conversation threads with unique thread IDs
   - Maintains persistent conversation history in SQLite database
   - Allows users to switch between multiple conversation sessions
   - Supports retrieving past conversations and continuing them seamlessly

4. **Extraction System**:
   - `baymax-backend/symptom_extraction.py`: Extracts primary symptoms and details from user messages
   - `baymax-backend/medication_extraction.py`: Processes medication information from uploaded images

5. **User Interfaces**:
   - Command-line interface (`baymax-backend/main.py`)
   - Web interface using Gradio (`baymax-backend/main_gradio.py`)
   - Expo mobile application (`baymax-frontend/`)
   - Both backend interfaces support thread management for persistent conversations

6. **RAG pipeline** (`disease_prediction_rag.py`)
    - Based on provided disease summary in 2-3 sentence it will predict the possible diseases in oder of likelyhood.
    - Qdrant is used as Vectore DataBase.
    - Uses Hybrid Retriever (bm25_retriever + vector_retriever) With Rerank (cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2)
    - select LLM_TYPE in .env for processing query :llama3-70b-8192 by groq or gemini-1.5-flash by google (groq is recommanded for low latency)
    - Use text_disease_prediction_rag.py for more details.

## Features

- **Structured Symptom Documentation**: Collects symptom details including severity, duration, location, and more
- **Multi-symptom Management**: Tracks multiple symptoms in a single session
- **Thread ID Tracking**: Maintains persistent conversation threads across sessions
- **Medication Label Processing**: Extracts medication information from uploaded images  
- **Voice Conversation Processing**: Extracts symptoms from transcribed patient-doctor conversations
- **Interactive Web Interface**: User-friendly Gradio interface with symptom tracking display
- **Thread Management**: Switch between different conversation threads or start new ones


## Health Timeline Feature

The Baymax app includes a comprehensive Health Timeline feature that provides users with a complete view of their entire health history across all conversations. This powerful feature enables tracking symptoms, medications, and conversations over time, regardless of which conversation thread they originated from.

### Key Features

#### Cross-Conversation Health History

- **Complete Timeline**: All health data is aggregated across all conversation threads
- **Thread Context**: Each item shows which conversation it originated from
- **Chronological View**: Data is sorted by date, with the most recent items appearing first

#### Advanced Search and Filtering

The Health Timeline includes powerful search and filtering capabilities:

1. **Text Search**:
   - Search symptoms by name, location, characteristics, and triggers
   - Search medications by name, instructions, strength, and prescriber
   - Search conversations by content and thread title

2. **Time Range Filtering**:
   - All time
   - Today
   - Last 7 days
   - Last 30 days
   - Last year

3. **Specialized Filters**:
   - **Symptoms**: Filter by severity (Mild, Moderate, Moderate to Severe, Severe, Very Severe)
   - **Medications**: Filter by status (Active, Discontinued)
   - **Conversations**: Filter by message type (All, User messages, Baymax responses)

To verify the Health Timeline functionality, create multiple conversation threads with Baymax, discuss different symptoms and medications in each thread, then navigate to the Health History tab to confirm that data from all threads appears properly.

## How to Use

### Command Line Interface

1. Run `python baymax-backend/main.py` to start the command-line interface
2. Interact with the agent by describing your symptoms
3. Use special commands:
   - Type `exit`, `quit`, or `bye` to end the conversation
   - Type `threads` to list active conversation threads
   - Type `thread <thread_id>` to switch to a specific thread
   - Type `summary` to get a summary of documented symptoms
   - Type `voice` to process a voice conversation file

### Gradio Web Interface

1. Run `python baymax-backend/main_gradio.py` to start the web interface
2. Access the interface at http://127.0.0.1:7860
3. Use the conversation panel to interact with the agent
4. Features:
   - View current symptoms in the right panel
   - Start a new conversation with the ""New Conversation"" button
   - Select previous threads from the dropdown menu
   - View thread information including creation time and message count

### Expo Frontend

1. Navigate to the `baymax-frontend` directory.
2. Run `npm install` (if you haven't already or if dependencies have changed).
3. Run `npm start` to start the Expo development server.
4. Use the Expo Go app on your mobile device to connect to the development server.
5. Alternatively, use an emulator to test the application.

### FastAPI Server

The project includes a FastAPI server that can be run for serving the langgraph agent for the expo client.

1. Ensure you are in the project's root directory or can correctly reference the path to `app.py`.
2. Run the server using the command:
   `python baymax-backend/app.py`


## Requirements

- Python 3.10+ (for backend)
- Node.js and npm (for frontend)
- OpenAI API key (set as an environment variable `OPENAI_API_KEY`)
- Set environment variables LLM_TYPE (groq or google), GEMINI_API_KEY, GROQ_API_KEY based on llm_type
- Setup Qdrant in docker container


## Setup

1. Clone this repository
2. Install backend dependencies:
   `pip install -r baymax-backend/requirements.txt` (assuming your requirements file is in the backend folder, please adjust if it's in the root or elsewhere)
3. Install frontend dependencies:
   `cd baymax-frontend && npm install`
4. Set up your OpenAI API key as an environment variable (`OPENAI_API_KEY`) for the backend to function correctly.
5. Run the applications using the instructions in the ""How to Use"" section.

## Documents

The following documents provide detailed information about the project's architecture, data schema, and use cases:

1. [Data Schema](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sBzSR1jdjC9XWkTIZaqrUFHMIwtgNfVxq3LY2ljImIs/edit?tab=t.0) - Defines the structured data format for symptom documentation

2. [AgentX-Health Kick-Off Meeting](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1eDQMryUz0kMzt4K9KkPQC3Ob2OLHeLvfEyREmZyHK1I/edit?tab=t.0) - Overview of the project's initial planning and goals

3. [Functionality of Components and Use Case Mapping](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1iXce2qyz664qGSdg08gIdYjVfgOmVt2yFk3YHXHGAIs/edit?tab=t.0) - Detailed breakdown of component functionality and use case implementation
